text("The Value of Boredom", "health", """
We live in an age that treats boredom as a problem to be solved. The moment a queue slows down or a conversation falters, most of us reach for a phone. Entire industries compete to fill every spare second of our attention, and they are remarkably good at it. It is now possible to go through an entire day without experiencing a single minute of unstructured, unoccupied time.

Yet a number of psychologists argue that boredom performs a useful function. It is, in their view, a signal: an uncomfortable feeling that tells us our current activity is not meeting our needs and encourages us to look for something more meaningful. When the signal is constantly silenced by quick distractions, we may lose the push that leads us towards more rewarding goals.

Boredom has also been linked to creativity. In one frequently cited experiment, participants who had first completed a deliberately dull task, copying numbers from a telephone directory, later produced more imaginative answers on a creative thinking test than those who had not. The researchers suggested that a bored mind tends to wander, and that this wandering can lead to unexpected connections between ideas. Many writers and scientists have described their best insights arriving not at their desks but on long walks, in the shower or while staring out of a train window.

This does not mean that all boredom is beneficial. Chronic boredom, particularly at work or school, is associated with poor concentration, low mood and risky behaviour. The difference seems to lie in whether we have the freedom to respond to the feeling. Short periods of boredom, followed by the chance to choose what to do next, may be valuable. Long periods in which we are trapped in meaningless activity are not.

Perhaps the most practical conclusion is a modest one. Rather than eliminating every empty moment, we might occasionally allow ourselves to sit with the discomfort and see where our thoughts take us.
""", [("falter", "słabnąć, rwać się"), ("unstructured", "niezorganizowany"), ("silence", "uciszać"), ("dull", "nudny"), ("wander", "błądzić (o myślach)"), ("insight", "wgląd, olśnienie"), ("chronic", "przewlekły"), ("sit with", "pozostać z (uczuciem)")],
[mc("In the first paragraph, the writer suggests that modern life", ["leaves very little time unoccupied.", "has made people more patient.", "is more boring than in the past.", "has reduced the use of phones."], "leaves very little time unoccupied.", "Można przejść cały dzień bez minuty nieustrukturyzowanego czasu.", {"has made people more patient.": "Przeciwnie – sięgamy po telefon natychmiast.", "is more boring than in the past.": "Tekst mówi odwrotnie.", "has reduced the use of phones.": "Brak takiej informacji."}),
 mc("Some psychologists see boredom as", ["a sign that we need something more meaningful.", "a symptom of illness.", "a result of using phones.", "a problem that industries should solve."], "a sign that we need something more meaningful.", "'a signal… encourages us to look for something more meaningful'.", {"a symptom of illness.": "Nie w tym akapicie.", "a result of using phones.": "Telefony ją tłumią, a nie powodują.", "a problem that industries should solve.": "To pogląd krytykowany przez autora."}),
 tf("In the experiment, people who did a dull task first gave more creative answers.", "True", "Akapit 3."),
 tf("The experiment was repeated with the same results in several countries.", "Not Given", "Tekst nie mówi o powtórzeniach eksperymentu."),
 mc("What distinguishes harmful boredom from useful boredom, according to the text?", ["Whether people are free to respond to it.", "Whether it happens at home or at work.", "How old the person is.", "Whether it leads to creativity."], "Whether people are free to respond to it.", "'The difference seems to lie in whether we have the freedom to respond'.", {"Whether it happens at home or at work.": "Praca jest przykładem, nie kryterium.", "How old the person is.": "Wiek nie jest wspomniany.", "Whether it leads to creativity.": "Kreatywność to skutek, nie kryterium."}),
 tf("The writer recommends removing all distractions from daily life.", "False", "Konkluzja jest 'modest' – czasem pozwolić sobie na nudę.")])

text("Who Owns a Language?", "culture", """
English is now spoken by more people as a second or foreign language than as a first language. This simple fact has profound consequences. For centuries, the norms of English were set by a relatively small number of native speakers in Britain and, later, in North America. Dictionaries, grammar books and examination boards looked to these communities to decide what counted as correct. Today, the majority of conversations in English take place between people for whom it is not a mother tongue: a Brazilian engineer negotiating with a Korean supplier, or a Polish student chatting online with a classmate in Kenya.

Some linguists argue that this shift calls for a new attitude towards correctness. In international settings, they point out, what matters most is not whether a speaker sounds like a native, but whether they can make themselves understood. Certain features that are considered errors in traditional teaching, such as omitting the third-person 's' in 'she work', rarely cause misunderstanding. Others, such as the misplaced stress in a key word, can lead to genuine confusion. From this perspective, teachers should concentrate on the features that actually affect communication.

Not everyone is persuaded. Critics worry that abandoning established standards could leave learners at a disadvantage, particularly in formal contexts such as job applications or academic writing, where conventional accuracy is still expected and judged. Many learners themselves say that they want to master standard forms, even if they do not intend to sound British or American. For them, accuracy is a mark of competence and a source of confidence.

Perhaps the answer lies in flexibility rather than in choosing one model. Skilled communicators adjust their language to their audience: they may use a more relaxed, internationally intelligible English in a quick business call, and a more carefully standard form in a written report. Ownership of English, in other words, may belong not to any single group of speakers, but to anyone who uses it with awareness and skill.
""", [("profound", "głęboki"), ("norm", "norma"), ("mother tongue", "język ojczysty"), ("omit", "pomijać"), ("misplaced stress", "błędnie postawiony akcent"), ("persuaded", "przekonany"), ("at a disadvantage", "w gorszym położeniu"), ("intelligible", "zrozumiały")],
[mc("What point does the writer make in the first paragraph?", ["Most English conversations now involve non-native speakers.", "Native speakers no longer use dictionaries.", "English is losing popularity worldwide.", "Examination boards ignore native speakers."], "Most English conversations now involve non-native speakers.", "'the majority of conversations in English take place between people for whom it is not a mother tongue'.", {"Native speakers no longer use dictionaries.": "Brak takiej informacji.", "English is losing popularity worldwide.": "Przeciwnie.", "Examination boards ignore native speakers.": "Historycznie było odwrotnie."}),
 tf("Some linguists believe intelligibility matters more than sounding native.", "True", "Akapit 2."),
 tf("Omitting the third-person 's' frequently causes serious misunderstanding.", "False", "'rarely cause misunderstanding'."),
 mc("Critics are concerned that changing standards could", ["harm learners in formal situations.", "make English harder to learn.", "reduce the number of teachers.", "favour British English over American."], "harm learners in formal situations.", "'leave learners at a disadvantage, particularly in formal contexts'.", {"make English harder to learn.": "Nie wspomniano.", "reduce the number of teachers.": "Nie wspomniano.", "favour British English over American.": "Nie o to chodzi."}),
 tf("Most learners want to sound exactly like British or American speakers.", "False", "Chcą opanować standard, 'even if they do not intend to sound British or American'."),
 mc("The writer's conclusion is that", ["good communicators adapt their English to the situation.", "one international standard should replace native norms.", "native speakers should decide what is correct.", "accuracy is no longer important."], "good communicators adapt their English to the situation.", "Ostatni akapit – elastyczność.", {"one international standard should replace native norms.": "Autor odrzuca wybór jednego modelu.", "native speakers should decide what is correct.": "Własność języka nie należy do jednej grupy.", "accuracy is no longer important.": "W raporcie liczy się staranny standard."})])
